    "answer": "According to the coursebook, benchmarking is a method that enables specific comparisons between companies and selects the best one as a reference in order to optimize the company's performance. These comparisons help an organization better understand itself, identify superior methods and practices used by other organizations, and allow certain processes to be adapted.\n\nCompetitive benchmarking specifically involves comparing with companies that operate in the same industry and service the same or similar markets. Important figures or indicators are compared — for example, most car manufacturers provide new models to competitors to compare technical development, features, and handling.\n\nAdvantages of competitive benchmarking:\n- It provides organizations with a clear picture of their position in the market relative to direct competitors.\n\nDisadvantages of competitive benchmarking:\n- It requires much preparation work.\n- It requires open communication between competitors in order to be effective — participants must give and receive an equal amount of information, which can be difficult to ensure.\n- Competitive information may be limited or not available at all.\n- The information gained has no novelty and does not contribute much to innovation or address future trends and developments, since the data concerns existing products and known competitors.\n\nGiven the internal problems the diet food producer is facing, competitive benchmarking may not be the most suitable option at this time. The coursebook suggests that internal benchmarking — performed within the organization itself — would be more appropriate in this case. The data, figures, and numbers are readily available internally, making the process faster and less resource-intensive. The comparison of indicators within the same organization is more straightforward since the processes and structures are similar. However, it is important to be aware that managers might feel that their management practices are being evaluated rather than processes, which could be seen as open criticism and compromise working relationships. This risk must be managed carefully during the process."
